.\" setreuid.2 - set the real and effective user or group ID
.TH SETREUID 2 "2026-10-05" "Substrate" "System Calls Manual"
.SH NAME
setreuid, setregid \- set the real and effective user or group ID
.SH LIBRARY
.B libc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <unistd.h>
.PP
.BI "int setreuid(uid_t " ruid ", uid_t " euid ");"
.br
.BI "int setregid(gid_t " rgid ", gid_t " egid ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR setreuid ()
sets the real user ID of the calling process to
.I ruid
and its effective user ID to
.IR euid .
.BR setregid ()
does the same for the real and effective group IDs.
An argument of \-1 leaves the corresponding ID unchanged.
.PP
A process whose effective user ID is 0 may set the IDs to any values.
Any other process may set the real ID only to its current real or
effective ID, and the effective ID only to its current real, effective or
saved ID.
.PP
If the real ID is changed, or the effective ID is set to a value that is
not the real ID, the saved ID is set to the new effective ID.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, 0 is returned.  On error, \-1 is returned, and
.I errno
is set appropriately; no ID is changed.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EPERM
The process is not privileged and asked for an ID it is not allowed to
take.
.SH EXAMPLE
Swap the real and effective user IDs, and swap them back:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int
main(void)
{
    uid_t ruid = getuid(), euid = geteuid();

    if (setreuid(euid, ruid) == \-1) {
        perror("setreuid");
        return 1;
    }
    /* ... the invoking user's ID is now the effective one ... */
    if (setreuid(ruid, euid) == \-1) {
        perror("setreuid");
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR setuid (2),
.BR seteuid (2),
.BR setresuid (2)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2001, POSIX.1-2008, 4.3BSD.
